MetaRefreshAttributeSanitizer checks the URL of a <meta http-equiv="refresh"> directive only when its content starts with a refresh time. Since the fix for CVE-2026-121373, only the ASCII whitespace of the HTML specification could come before that time. Chromium-based browsers also skip a vertical tab and the Unicode spaces U+1680, U+2000 to U+200A, U+2028, U+205F and U+3000.

When the time followed one of these characters, the sanitizer returned the value unchanged. Its URL wasn't checked against allowLinkHosts() and allowLinkSchemes(), and Chromium followed it:

$sanitizer->sanitizeFor('head', "<meta http-equiv=\"refresh\" content=\"\u{3000}0;url=https://evil.example/\">");
// returned unchanged, and Chromium redirects to https://evil.example/

The vertical tab is a regression of the fix for CVE-2026-121373, since the expression used before matched it. The Unicode spaces have been acce

When it denormalizes data into a class, the Serializer computes which attributes it may write from the #[Ignore] and #[Groups] metadata. It did that for the requested class, but wrote into another class in two cases. The first case is the class that a discriminator map picks from the data. The second is the class of the object passed with the object_to_populate or deep_object_to_populate option.

When the requested class has no #[Ignore] attribute and the context sets no groups, nothing was restricted. A client could then set a property that the picked class marks with #[Ignore], constructor arguments included:

#[DiscriminatorMap(typeProperty: 'kind', mapping: ['email' => EmailNotification::class])]
abstract class Notification {}

class EmailNotification extends Notification
{
    #[Ignore]
    public bool $skipModeration = false;
}

