The fixes for CVE-2026-135804 (GHSA-cpww-5vpx-72x7) bound the regex work of a JsonCrawler::find() call, and evaluate filters and absolute queries once per call. Chained recursive descent isn't bounded. Each ..* segment takes the descendants of every node selected so far. A node nested in other selected nodes is counted once per such ancestor. So the size of the node list is a power of the depth, one power per segment. RFC 9535 allows these duplicates.

$document = 'null';
for ($i = 0; $i < 40; ++$i) {
    $document = '{"a":'.$document.',"b":1}';
}

// 484 bytes
(new JsonCrawler($document))->find('$..*..*..*');    // 19760 nodes, 0.42 s, 35 MB
(new JsonCrawler($document))->find('$..*..*..*..*'); // 182780 nodes, 77 s, 256 MB

Each additional ..* multiplies the time and the memory. Other queries hold the process the same way, because work that doesn't depend on the node is repeated for eac
